# Optimal Non-Pharmaceutical Intervention Strategies for Minimising Disease Burden in Stochastic and Age-Structured Epidemic Models

Abstract: 
Early in the COVID-19 epidemic, before vaccines were available, limiting the spread of infection
depended on non-pharmaceutical interventions (NPIs). NPIs are interventions like lockdowns
and can only be sustained for a limited time. This raises the question of how best to deploy a
limited amount of intervention to reduce the total final number of infections or fatalities.
For a deterministic, homogeneous SIR model the answer is known. Under a fixed intervention
budget and maximum intervention, the total number of infections is minimised by a single
constant-level intervention started at one optimal time. The question remains how far that
result carries over to more realistic settings. Two models are explored in this report. The first
introduces randomness in a finite population, together with a possible influx of infections from
outside. The second adds age structure, splitting the population into a younger and an older
group and measuring fatalities. A central question is how to optimally time an intervention in
these models.
We find that the deterministic timing remains a good guide whenever the epidemic is certain
to take off. The epidemic almost always takes off when the initial number of infected individuals
is large enough and whenever infection is continually reintroduced from outside. This guide
breaks down only when take-off is itself uncertain. In an isolated population seeded by a single
infection an early intervention can prevent the epidemic from taking off. In this case the best
strategy is to act immediately. This effect is fragile and concerns the typical outcome only;
when guarding against the worst cases, a timing close to the deterministic optimum applies, in
large populations.
With age structure the decision is not only when to intervene but how to divide the budget
between contacts within each group and contacts between them. Protecting the small,
high-mortality elderly group is cheap. Therefore, most fatalities are prevented by an early,
long-sustained restriction of contacts within the elderly group and between the two groups. Re-
stricting contacts in the big low mortality younger group is less efficient at minimising fatalities.
For this group a short intervention starting close to the herd immunity threshold (similar to the
deterministic optimum) is selected by the optimisation algorithm.


Lena M Weyer
Master's Thesis
Biostatistics and Data Science (KI, KTH, SU)
2026

## Requirements

- R version 4.6.1
- Required packages: socialmixr, adaptivetau, deSolve, parallel 


## How to run

Run the scripts in order:

1. `run_code.R ` – defines paths and loads functions
2. Experiment you want to run, run 1. again before running next experiment.

Make sure folders data will be saved in in experiment exists.

